# Source redundancy, third batch: the cases that take redundant_page to 125 (75 cases).
#
# The first 16 are in `retrieval.jsonl` and the next 34 in `retrieval-b.jsonl`; both stay
# untouched so their published runs reproduce exactly.
#
# WHY A THIRD BATCH, and it is not the same reason as memory_write's. That point was blocked
# only by sample size. This one is blocked by sample size AND by label balance: of its 50
# cases only 18 are labelled `drop`, and `drop` is the acted direction, so even a perfect
# derivation had at most 18 acted cases to work with when a 90 % precision target needs 35.
# Adding cases in the existing proportion would not have helped. These 75 are 51 `drop` and
# 24 `keep` on purpose, which takes the point to 125 cases and 69 acted labels - enough for a
# 90 % target with room to spare, and the first point here that could reach for 95 %.
#
# THE CRITERION, verbatim from the question, because four labels were corrected in the
# second batch for drifting from it: drop only if `text` carries NO new figure, date, name,
# outcome, qualification or source that `known` lacks.
#
# Two readings of that sentence have to be pinned down or the labels are noise, and both were
# learned the hard way:
#
# 1. OFF-PURPOSE IS NOT REDUNDANT. A page with no bearing on the purpose is triage's problem,
#    and triage runs first; `decide_redundancy` is never handed a relevance signal at all.
#    Four cases in the second batch were labelled `drop` on the theory that an unrelated page
#    "adds nothing" and had to be corrected. rd-113 and rd-118 are that shape, labelled keep.
# 2. A NEW OUTLET IS NOT A NEW SOURCE. Twenty sites reprinting one agency wire carry one
#    source. A new source means a new PRIMARY one: a different body that counted, measured or
#    ruled on its own. rd-104 and rd-121 are the hard pair - the same figures as `known`, but
#    arrived at independently, which is corroboration and therefore keep.
#
# THE FAMILIES OF `drop`, chosen because they are what a real retriever actually returns:
# the wire copy, the aggregator with a new headline, the encyclopedic entry, the summary that
# rounds a figure down to less precision than `known` already has, the press release the
# report itself issued, and the database row.
#
# Annotator 1 (the author) labelled every case before any decision was run; annotator 2 is a
# separate generative model, blind to the first label and to the evaluator's answer.
